<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<kcfg xmlns="http://www.kde.org/standards/kcfg/1.0"
      xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
      xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.kde.org/standards/kcfg/1.0
      http://www.kde.org/standards/kcfg/1.0/kcfg.xsd">
  <kcfgfile name=""/>
  <group name="General">
    <!-- A plain image file, or a MoOS wallpaper package dir (MoOSUI2Graphite /
         MoOSUI2Tide) — moos-theme and moos-apply-theme write it per UI2 half.
         Empty means: follow the active palette (Graphite dark / Tide light). -->
    <entry name="Image" type="String">
      <default></default>
    </entry>
    <!-- The three-level motion policy: 0 still, 1 gentle, 2 alive.
             0 still  — nothing loops. One-shot entrance and value transitions
                        are off too, so an accessibility profile gets a
                        completely static desktop.
             1 gentle — the calm default: the ambient crossfade below, the
                        once-per-minute colon pulse, the rolling digits, the
                        weather art's slow bob. Nothing runs at a 100% duty
                        cycle.
             2 alive  — gentle PLUS the card sheen sweep, the system beacon's
                        ripple and the metric rings' halo. Still bounded: every
                        looping animation rests, because an infinite QML
                        animation pins the render loop at the full frame rate
                        and repaints the WHOLE window for as long as it runs —
                        about 11% of a CPU core whatever the item's size.

         THE DEFAULT IS -1 AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. -1 is a sentinel meaning
         "nobody has chosen a level on this desktop yet". KConfigXT cannot tell
         an absent key from a key that happens to hold the default value, so a
         default of 1 would have silently promoted every ALREADY-INSTALLED
         desktop — which has AmbientMotion=false written by an older moos-theme
         and no MotionMode at all — from "motion off" back to "gentle" the first
         time this key shipped. On -1 the scene falls back to AmbientMotion, so
         an upgrade never overrides a choice the user already made. moos-theme
         and the wallpaper config page both write a real 0/1/2, and from then on
         MotionMode is the answer.

         There is deliberately no range constraint here either. A min/max pair
         looks like the obvious safety net and is the opposite: KConfigXT CLAMPS
         an out-of-range value, so a corrupt "7" would silently arrive as 2 — the
         most expensive level, chosen by nobody. Left unconstrained, the raw
         value reaches main.qml, which accepts only 0, 1 or 2 and treats anything
         else as "unset" and falls back to AmbientMotion. Rejecting junk beats
         rounding it. -->
    <entry name="MotionMode" type="Int">
      <default>-1</default>
    </entry>
    <!-- The original Boolean, kept as MotionMode's backward-compatible mirror:
         0 <-> false, 1 and 2 <-> true. moos-theme still writes it in the same
         transaction as MotionMode, and every installed desktop still has it, so
         it is read whenever MotionMode is the -1 sentinel above. When both are
         set, MotionMode wins.

         What it gates at level 1: a low-duty-cycle ambient crossfade — 1.8
         seconds of work every 90 seconds, never a continuous full-screen
         animation. -->
    <entry name="AmbientMotion" type="Bool">
      <default>true</default>
    </entry>
    <!-- The bento dashboard below the icons.
         MEASURED COST, and the reason this switch exists. On a machine with no
         GPU every pixel is rasterised on the CPU, and the bento is expensive to
         rasterise: on the maintainer's VPS, with a 60s window after 70s of
         settling, plasmashell's rasteriser threads ran at

             bento visible   158%     bento hidden   0%     (A/B/A, both ways)

         and that is with motion fully off — forcing motionEnabled to false still
         left 124%, so the animations are only ~34 of those points. The bento is
         costly PER FRAME and something else in plasmashell keeps asking for
         frames; hiding it makes each frame cheap.

         On a GPU this is close to free, which is why the desktop editions keep it
         and only the cloud edition turns it off (build.sh, is_cloud). Anyone can
         turn it back on. -->
    <entry name="ShowDashboard" type="Bool">
      <default>true</default>
    </entry>
  </group>
</kcfg>
